      "writtenOn": "2022-06-03T05:06:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In general, the test runnable pattern is slightly harder to read than if we inlined it:\n\n```\npublic void testStartPlatformVpnMobilityWithMobike() {\n    setupAndVerifyPlatformVpn(ikeConfig);\n    \n    IkeSessionCallback ikeCb \u003d getIkeCb();\n    ChildSessionCallback childCb \u003d getChildCb();\n    NetworkCallback nwCb \u003d getNwCb();\n    \n    ...\n}\n```\n\nFYI: the pattern you\u0027re using of the test runnables is a very common functional programming pattern, called \"Continuation Passing Style\" or CPS for short. Unfortunately, because Java isn\u0027t a functional programming language at it\u0027s core, it\u0027s a little out of place.\n\nFYI2: The reason why we use this in CTS so much is that it allows us to have a try/finally to make 100% sure we clean up global device state. But given that unit tests by definition don\u0027t impact global state, the readability tradeoff isn\u0027t worth the \"safety\" guarantees.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1543,
        "startChar": 22,
